{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfiivukYwZEroZCBvWgNm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate, `asymmetric_trick_coin_motif_suspicion`, and it passed the self-check. The check's short fit gave an ELPD-LOO of -2107.4. The incumbent's is -2105.7, but that comes from a full fit, so the two aren't directly comparable yet.\n\nThe check raised two warnings that admission's full fit will judge for real:\n- **Reliability of the score:** 6.7% of trials had high Pareto-k values (the tolerance is 1%), so PSIS-LOO flagged the score as possibly inaccurate. I expect this comes from the incumbent's per-person parameters, which this model keeps unchanged, but I haven't checked the incumbent's own figure.\n- **Convergence:** this wasn't verified, because a one-chain smoke fit can't report R-hat.\n\n**What it refines:** the incumbent, `person_specific_motif_suspicion`.\n\n**The one change:** in the incumbent, people suspect a \"trick coin\" equally likely to favour heads or tails. Here that suspicion can lean one way: the trick coin's heads rate has a prior of Beta(2m, 2(1−m)), with m fitted. At m = 0.5 this is the incumbent's uniform prior again. If people expect rigged coins to favour heads, a head-heavy sequence is more easily explained as rigged and looks less random than an equally lopsided tail-heavy one. Everything else is unchanged:\n- each person's own belief about how often a fair coin switches,\n- each person's own weight on the repeating-motif generator,\n- each person's own decisiveness,\n- the per-flip length normalisation.\n\n**Why this change:** it targets the strongest critique that survives the multiple-testing correction. People chose the sequence with more heads 47.3% of the time, against 48.8% predicted by the incumbent, which treats heads and tails the same.\n\n**Novelty risk:** the effect is small, about 1.5 percentage points. If the fit puts m near 0.5, the predictions could land within the 0.002 novelty threshold of the incumbent and be rejected as a near-duplicate. I didn't look at the fitted value of m.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_3/`:\n- hypothesis.md\n- model_name.txt\n- candidate.py"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2780,"cache_read_input_tokens":76421,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2780,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3890b1d3-662e-4f05-bacc-5346312476ac","uuid":"f416e2ef-66ed-4dd4-a7f5-4a2d057ca5f2","timestamp":"2026-10-05T07:49:49.018Z","request_id":"req_011CfiivuKFwqPPhMJ1CV17Y"}
